(* ==========================================================================
   catalogue.bnf — WHAT NAMES EXIST.

   `grammar.bnf` says the SHAPE of a program; this says the NAMES one may
   write. Two data files and one compiler — this is the second file.

   Reference: rust-analyzer's `rust.ungram` — a data file beside the compiler,
   with a test that fails when the Rust drifts from it. Not invented here.

   # What is data and what is not

   A row has two halves and only one of them can live in a file:

     - DATA      the name written after `io.`, the extensions it accepts, and
                 which capabilities it declares. All of it is here.
     - BEHAVIOUR the function that turns bytes into rows or into types. A file
                 cannot carry code, so the implementing crate supplies it and
                 the loader matches it BY NAME.

   That split is rustc's `Providers`: the data says what exists, the code
   registers what it can do. It also buys a diagnostic the tree cannot make
   today — a host that cannot link SHACL currently just lacks the row, so
   `io.shacl("x.ttl")` reports «not an installed row», which is what a typo
   reports too. With the halves separate, "declared but not linked here" and
   "no such name" are different answers.

   # Status

   The rows below ARE the statics. `cargo xtask catalogue` generates
   `crates/fossil-base/src/providers/generated.rs` and
   `crates/fossil-descriptors-output/src/generated.rs` from this file, and
   `cargo xtask catalogue --check` fails when the checked-in output is stale.
   No Rust states a row a second time, so there is nothing left to drift.

   Which of the two files a row lands in is DERIVED, not configured, and it is
   the DATA/BEHAVIOUR split above: a row with a `decodes` needs a crate that can
   link a shape-language parser, so it is emitted into `fossil-descriptors-output`;
   every other row into `fossil-base`, which the compiler always has. That is
   also what makes `DATA` — the default of `System::providers` — exactly the
   `fossil-base` half.

   Generating rather than comparing is what makes a row's BEHAVIOUR half
   checkable: `decodes decode_shex` becomes a Rust PATH in the generated file,
   so a row naming a decoder nobody wrote is a build failure, not a declared
   capability with no behaviour behind it.
   ========================================================================== *)

(* ---------------------------------------------------------------- io, data *)
(* Rows that read ROWS. `reads` names how the bytes become a relation:
   `native <fn>` is a DuckDB table function, `materialised` means something
   outside the reader produces the relation and the core only scans it.

   The `native <fn>` token is the ONLY spelling of which reader a row uses.
   `NativeReader`'s variant name is derived from it — strip `read_`, upper-camel
   the rest — and `NativeReader::table_function` gives the token back, so nothing
   downstream re-states it.

   `rdf`'s extension list is kept in lockstep with the RDF provider's own.

   `signature` is what a program gets if it writes the constructor in a VALUE
   position — `io.csv("u.csv")` inside a mapping rather than as a binding. It
   was stated twice: here, and again as a hand-written row in
   `fossil_hir::stdlib`, and the two sets disagreed. The registry carried three
   (`csv`, `json`, `parquet`) against this file's six, so `io.rdf` — a source a
   program may bind like any other — had no signature at all and no diagnostic
   when misused. One clause, one place.

   `shex` and `shacl` below carry NO `signature`, and that is an answer rather
   than an omission: they give back a shape document, and `SigTy` has no
   spelling for one. Writing `-> Rows` for them to fill the column would be the
   mistake this fold exists to undo, one level along — three constructors of
   relations once declared that they returned a string, because the helper that
   built them took a scalar type and nobody noticed the row was lying. *)

row csv     = extensions "csv"                    ; reads native read_csv_auto
            ; signature (uri: String) -> Rows ; op Source .
row json    = extensions "json"                   ; reads native read_json_auto
            ; signature (uri: String) -> Rows ; op Source .
row parquet = extensions "parquet"                ; reads native read_parquet
            ; signature (uri: String) -> Rows ; op Source .
row rdf     = extensions "ttl" "nt" "n3" "rdf"    ; reads materialised
            ; signature (uri: String) -> Rows ; op Source .

(* --------------------------------------------------------------- io, types *)
(* Rows that read TYPES. `decodes` names the function the implementing crate
   registers; there is no `native` here, because a shape language is decoded in
   Rust and never by the database.

   `rdf` and `shacl` both accept `.ttl` and that is not a conflict: the name
   decides. `io.rdf("g.ttl")` reads rows out of a graph, `io.shacl("s.ttl")`
   reads types out of a shapes graph — two capabilities over one syntax, told
   apart by the name. Under dispatch by extension that pair is unexpressible.

   `shex` does NOT claim `.ttl`, and that is the same rule read the other way:
   `io.shex("catalogue.ttl")` is an error naming the constructor and the
   extension, where before the extension alone chose the row and the constructor
   written in the program was decorative. *)

row shex    = extensions "shex" "shexj" "shexc"   ; decodes decode_shex         .
row shacl   = extensions "ttl" "shacl"            ; decodes decode_shacl        .

(* ------------------------------------------------------------- NOT IN HERE *)
(* The WRITE half of the table is deliberately absent, not stubbed:
   `grammar.bnf` says «`io` names only the INPUT», and where a program writes
   to has no syntax at all. A row for it here would be a name no program can
   write.

   The stdlib catalogue used to be named here as the larger half that had not
   arrived. It is below. *)

(* ========================================================================== *)
(* THE STDLIB CATALOGUE — every function the language declares.
   ==========================================================================

   A `fn` row is four things and no more: a dotted NAME, a SIGNATURE, a RETURN
   type and a LOWERING.

   # What a row must NOT write

   The RECEIVER. `str.trim` hangs off a string and `seq.where` hangs off a
   relation, and both are DERIVED from the head of the dotted name by
   `fossil_hir::stdlib::split_receiver` — the one place the rule lives. A field
   a row fills by hand is a field that ends up disagreeing with the name beside
   it, which is the defect dispatch-by-receiver exists to end, one level down.
   So the grammar here cannot express a receiver at all.

   # The signature

     ( name: Type , name: Type+ , name = Type ) -> Type

   `:` is a position written by ORDER; `=` is a position written WITH ITS NAME
   (`grammar.bnf`, NamedArg). One row uses `=` today — `seq.join`'s `on` — and
   it is a property of the surface, not a convenience: `join(User, a == b)` is a
   different program from `join(User, on = a == b)`, and a binder that filled
   the slot from either spelling would widen the language by accident.

   `+` is one-or-more, `?` is zero-or-one, and bare is exactly one.

   Twelve type spellings: seven scalars — String Integer Float Bool Date
   DateTime SeqString — and five whose meaning depends on the RECEIVER, which is
   why they are not scalars: Rows (the relation a verb is a verb of), Predicate
   (a condition over its rows), Column and Binding (positions that NAME rather
   than evaluate), and Aggregate (whose argument names are the PROGRAM's).

   **The return type is independent of the lowering, and the grammar has to keep
   it that way.** `seq.count` is an operator that gives back an Integer;
   `io.csv` is an operator that takes a scalar and gives back Rows. A notation
   that tied `-> Rows` to `op` could express neither.

   # The lowering

   `expr "<template>"` is a scalar SQL expression with `%N` holes, N zero-based.
   The holes are INDEXED and not sequential because a hole may appear twice or
   not at all, and a template therefore does NOT determine arity — the signature
   does.

   `op <Variant>` names one operator of the algebra. The token is emitted as the
   Rust path `PlanOp::<Variant>`, so a row naming an operator nobody wrote is a
   build failure — the same guarantee `decodes decode_shex` buys above. `PlanOp`
   itself stays hand-written: `lower_source_stage` matches it exhaustively, and
   each variant carries what it lowers to, which is prose a token has nowhere to
   put.
   ========================================================================== *)

(* ------------------------------------------------------------------- core/ *)
(* This was eight rows. Six were RDF term constructors — `iri`, `triple`,
   `blank`, `literal`, `typed`, `emit` — and not one `.fossil` in the tree
   called any of them; they took the variadic and the not-an-argument template
   shapes with them, which is why the notation above needs neither.

   `core.require` was the eighth and is gone from the language. It read its
   argument twice around an `error()`, and `error()` has no DataFusion
   equivalent at all — there is no way to raise from an expression, so «this
   value or stop the run» had no spelling on the engine that writes a corpus. *)

fn core.lang = (value: String, tag: String) -> String ; expr "%0" .

(* -------------------------------------------------------------------- seq/ *)
(* The relation verbs, and a new verb is a ROW rather than a grammar rule —
   that is what having a receiver bought. `filter` and `project` are spelled
   `where` and `select` because the catalogue must spell what the surface
   spells: `members_of(Relation)` is what an IDE offers, and offering
   `User.filter(…)` for a language whose word is `where` is a completion that is
   confidently wrong.

   `map`'s function argument is the higher-order position the surface does not
   spell yet (`grammar.bnf` has no lambda), so the row declares the relation and
   stops. A parameter invented here would be the reference page's old invented
   `by`/`key`/`desc` written one layer lower down.

   `distinct` takes NOTHING. A `DISTINCT ON` column list keeps one row per group
   and chooses it arbitrarily without an `ORDER BY`, and `sort` has no lowering
   yet, so there is no way to write the order that would settle it — a verb
   whose corpus differs between two runs of the same program is not one this
   language can admit.

   `union`'s right side takes no `X as Y`, unlike a join's: the two sides become
   ONE row under the pipeline's name, so an alias would name a side the verb
   does not produce.

   `group_by` is the one row with two `+` positions, and they do not collide.
   The rule a repetition obeys is about POSITIONAL arguments — a repeating
   position cannot be told from the position after it — and `aggregations` is
   reached by NAME or not at all. `count` is the one `seq/` row that is not a
   verb of the algebra: it takes a relation and gives back a number. *)

fn seq.where    = (rows: Rows, keep: Predicate) -> Rows                 ; op Where    .
fn seq.map      = (rows: Rows) -> Rows                                  ; op Map      .
fn seq.flatten  = (rows: Rows, of: Column) -> Rows                      ; op Flatten  .
fn seq.take     = (rows: Rows, n: Integer) -> Rows                      ; op Take     .
fn seq.drop     = (rows: Rows, n: Integer) -> Rows                      ; op Drop     .
fn seq.distinct = (rows: Rows) -> Rows                                  ; op Distinct .
fn seq.sort     = (rows: Rows, by: Column+) -> Rows                     ; op Sort     .
fn seq.select   = (rows: Rows, cols: Column+) -> Rows                   ; op Select   .
fn seq.join     = (rows: Rows, right: Binding, on = Predicate) -> Rows  ; op Join     .
fn seq.union    = (rows: Rows, other: Binding) -> Rows                  ; op Union    .
fn seq.group_by = (rows: Rows, keys: Column+, aggregations: Aggregate+) -> Rows
                                                                        ; op GroupBy  .
fn seq.count    = (rows: Rows) -> Integer                               ; op Count    .

(* -------------------------------------------------------------------- str/ *)
(* Every operation on a string. There is no `clean/`: no principle separates
   cleaning a string from operating on one, so `trim`, `lower`, `upper`, `slug`
   and `strip_html` live here with the other eight.

   `normalize_unicode` is NOT among them and is not in the language: DuckDB has
   `nfc_normalize` and therefore NFC only, and a `normalize_unicode(x, 'NFKD')`
   that silently gave NFC would be a function that lies. *)

fn str.length      = (text: String) -> Integer                  ; expr "length(%0)" .
fn str.slice       = (text: String, start: Integer) -> String   ; expr "substring(%0, %1)" .
fn str.contains    = (text: String, needle: String) -> Bool     ; expr "contains(%0, %1)" .
fn str.starts_with = (text: String, prefix: String) -> Bool     ; expr "starts_with(%0, %1)" .
fn str.ends_with   = (text: String, suffix: String) -> Bool     ; expr "ends_with(%0, %1)" .
fn str.replace     = (text: String, needle: String, replacement: String) -> String
                                                                ; expr "replace(%0, %1, %2)" .
fn str.split       = (text: String, separator: String) -> SeqString
                                                                ; expr "string_split(%0, %1)" .
fn str.concat      = (text: String, other: String) -> String    ; expr "concat(%0, %1)" .
fn str.trim        = (text: String) -> String                   ; expr "trim(%0)" .
fn str.lower       = (text: String) -> String                   ; expr "lower(%0)" .
fn str.upper       = (text: String) -> String                   ; expr "upper(%0)" .

(* `str.slug` replaced the `fossil_slug` UDF, and THE TEMPLATE IT WAS SPECIFIED
   WITH MATCHED NEITHER RUST IMPLEMENTATION — measurement is the only reason we
   know. The tree carried two slug functions: the `fossil_slug` UDF, which was
   `slug::slugify`, and a hand-written one that kept `.`, lower-cased, and
   collapsed every run of non-`\p{L}\p{N}.-` to a single `-`. Against a 16-case
   hand corpus the specified `'[^a-z0-9.-]+'` form scored 8/16 against the first
   and 7/16 against the second — it is a hybrid of the two. Worse, it maps `ÅÄÖ`
   and `日本語` to the EMPTY STRING, which in a slug that ends up in an IRI is an
   identity collision and not a formatting difference.

   The form below is EXACT against that hand-written one: 16/16 by hand and 0
   mismatches over 240,998 fuzzed inputs. `slug::slugify` cannot be reproduced
   at all — it TRANSLITERATES (`Straße` → `strasse`, `€100` → `eur100`, `Москва`
   → `moskva`) and DuckDB ships no transliteration function. The best available
   approximation, over `strip_accents`, differs on 55.6% of Latin-accented
   input, so this keeps the implementation whose behaviour is expressible and
   says so. *)

fn str.slug = (text: String) -> String
            ; expr "trim(regexp_replace(lower(trim(%0)), '[^\p{L}\p{N}.-]', '-', 'g'), '-')" .

(* `str.strip_html` replaced `voca_rs::strip::strip_tags`, and it is the ONE
   template that is NOT exact: 27/29 by hand and 1,837 mismatches over 120,000
   fuzzed inputs (1.5%). `voca_rs` is a parser and this is a regex, so the
   residue is entirely MALFORMED markup — an unbalanced quote makes voca swallow
   the rest of the string and a regex cannot. With a quote character present the
   disagreement rate is 10.9%; without one it is 0.6%.

   The naive `'<[^>]*>'` scored 17/29. What this adds is the two things voca
   actually does: a bare `<` followed by whitespace is TEXT (`a < b` survives),
   and a quoted attribute may contain `>` (`<a href="x>y">link</a>` → `link`).

   It is also the template that ended the old line-scanning parser. It carries
   double quotes of its own, so under «the odd segments are literals» every
   later token on the line changed parity — a silently different catalogue
   rather than a parse error. `\"` is the escape; see `xtask::catalogue::lex`. *)

fn str.strip_html = (text: String) -> String
                  ; expr "regexp_replace(%0, '<(?:>|$|[^\s](?:\"[^\"]*\"|''[^'']*''|[^>\"''])*(?:>|$))', '', 'g')" .

(* ------------------------------------------------------------------ parse/ *)
(* Casts, and `strptime` behind two rows that differ by one.

   `decimal` returns Float and the OUTER cast is what makes that sentence true;
   the template was `CAST(%0 AS DECIMAL(38,18))`, which returns a fixed-point
   column while the row said `Float`. The INNER cast stays because it is the
   only thing separating this row from `parse.float`: `CAST('1e400' AS
   DECIMAL(38,18))` REFUSES the value where `CAST('1e400' AS DOUBLE)` yields
   `inf`. Measured on DuckDB v1.5.3.

   `strptime` returns a TIMESTAMP whatever the format string asks for, so a bare
   call was `parse.datetime` under a second name. The cast on `date` is what
   makes the two rows two things.

   `parse.json` and `parse.csv_row` are gone. `json` lowered to
   `json_extract_string`, which is DuckDB's — DataFusion ships no JSON
   extraction in its default function set, so the row could not run on the
   engine that writes a corpus. `csv_row` renders perfectly well and is simply
   not wanted: splitting a field out of a line of CSV is what `io.csv` is for,
   and a row that re-does the reader's job inside a mapping invites a program to
   parse its own input twice. It was the only user of `split_part`. *)

fn parse.integer  = (text: String) -> Integer                   ; expr "CAST(%0 AS BIGINT)" .
fn parse.float    = (text: String) -> Float                     ; expr "CAST(%0 AS DOUBLE)" .
fn parse.decimal  = (text: String) -> Float                     ; expr "CAST(CAST(%0 AS DECIMAL(38,18)) AS DOUBLE)" .
fn parse.date     = (text: String, format: String) -> Date      ; expr "CAST(strptime(%0, %1) AS DATE)" .
fn parse.datetime = (text: String, format: String) -> DateTime  ; expr "strptime(%0, %1)" .

(* ------------------------------------------------------------------- math/ *)
(* SIX rows, and NO `ceil`/`floor`.

   The four aggregates are NOT redundant with `seq.group_by`, and the shape of
   that mistake is worth writing down because it was nearly made: `group_by`'s
   aggregations are SPELLED with these rows —
   `group_by(Order.customer, total = math.sum(Order.amount))` — and
   `RegistryEntry::agg_fn` is the map from the row to the `AggFn` that
   `fossil_mir::lower` reads. Delete them and `group_by` keeps its syntax and
   loses its vocabulary: `aggregate_names()` returns nothing, every aggregation
   is refused with a message listing no alternatives, and `AggFn`'s four
   variants become states nothing can construct.

   They are unrenderable as SCALAR calls on DataFusion, which is a different
   claim and the one `UNREACHABLE_ON_DATAFUSION` makes: an aggregate in a
   property position is a pipeline with a group-by, not an expression.

   `round` is the only Float → Integer row, and the cast is what makes it one:
   `round(DOUBLE)` is a DOUBLE in DuckDB, so the bare call returned `2.0` under
   a row promising an integer. Rounding first and casting second is not a double
   rounding — `round` has already made the value integral — and half-away-from-
   zero survives it (`-1.5` → `-2`). Measured on DuckDB v1.5.3. *)

fn math.sum   = (value: Float) -> Float     ; expr "sum(%0)" .
fn math.avg   = (value: Float) -> Float     ; expr "avg(%0)" .
fn math.min   = (value: Float) -> Float     ; expr "min(%0)" .
fn math.max   = (value: Float) -> Float     ; expr "max(%0)" .
fn math.abs   = (value: Float) -> Float     ; expr "abs(%0)" .
fn math.round = (value: Float) -> Integer   ; expr "CAST(round(%0) AS BIGINT)" .

(* ------------------------------------------------ validate/ and anon/, gone *)
(* Neither namespace exists, and each left for its own reason.

   `validate/` was five rows sharing one shape — the value or an error, never a
   null — and that shape is `error()`, which DataFusion cannot express. There is
   no way to raise from an expression, so every one of the five was unrenderable
   on the engine that writes a corpus. `validate.regex` is the instructive one:
   it lowered to a bare `regexp_matches`, which renders happily as a BOOLEAN
   under a row declaring `String`, so a mapping that validated a code wrote the
   literal `true` into it and nothing failed. Renderable and wrong is worse than
   absent.

   `anon/` was two rows and it is a change of design, not of engine.
   Anonymisation is a DECLARED POLICY the writer applies, so the language names
   no anonymisation operator: a policy can be stated once for a corpus and
   audited, where `anon.hash(x, salt = "…")` puts the salt in the program text
   at every call site and makes «is this column anonymised?» a question you
   answer by reading every mapping. `anon.hmac` had already gone for a third
   reason — HMAC needs a key schedule and DuckDB has `sha256` and no HMAC.

   `anon.hash` was also the last consumer of `sha256`, which is why
   `crates/fossil-df/Cargo.toml` leaves `crypto_expressions` off. *)
